---
import BaseLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SessionsGrid from "../../components/SessionsGrid.astro";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../../site-config";

const description = `The twelve-week teaching schedule: what happens in each ${sessionLabels.singular.toLowerCase()}, what to prepare, and what leaves the room.`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${sessionLabels.plural} — ${siteConfig.name}`}
  description={description}
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <h1>{sessionLabels.plural}</h1>
  <p class="lead">{description}</p>
  <p>
    A Lab is where the week's material gets applied under supervision, in
    front of the other people in the room. Some weeks that means role-play —
    running an apology into the ground in front of a group who's watching
    for the exact moment it goes wrong. Some weeks it means a live edit — a
    600-word message read aloud and workshopped down to one sentence on the
    spot. Some weeks it's a shared, tightly capped grocery budget everyone
    has to defend cutting from. Bring the case study, expect a
    demonstration, leave with feedback on the one decision you were least
    sure about.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every Lab runs one hour, and every Lab runs the same hour underneath
    whatever it's about. The subject changes every week; the shape
    doesn't — twelve chances to get used to it, rather than twelve
    different formats to learn:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A five-minute introduction, tying back to the previous week</li>
    <li>A first activity</li>
    <li>A presentation or class discussion of it</li>
    <li>A second activity</li>
    <li>A discussion or debrief of that</li>
    <li>A five-minute wrap-up</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    No laptops, phones, or other screens are used in any Lab, ever. A
    computer science student going a full hour without checking one is not
    a bit this course invented — it is, by reputation, rare enough to be
    worth scheduling on purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Freeman, S., Eddy, S. L., McDonough, M., Smith, M. K., Okoroafor, N.,
    Jordt, H., & Wenderoth, M. P. (2014) found active-learning formats
    measurably outperform straight lecturing on exam performance across
    STEM courses — the reason this course treats the Lab as one half of a
    pair, not an optional add-on: the lecture sets up the research, the
    definitions, and the "how"; the Lab that same week is where it actually
    gets used.
  </p>
  <SessionsGrid />
</BaseLayout>
